And sure enough, it did help.

261
00:12:28,680 --> 00:12:30,630
And we've run a number of studies

262
00:12:30,630 --> 00:12:32,310
and I could talk to you about our successes,

263
00:12:32,310 --> 00:12:34,770
but actually the most important study we ever did

264
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:37,350
was the one that had the least impact.

265
00:12:37,350 --> 00:12:38,970
And it was the only time

266
00:12:38,970 --> 00:12:42,210
we did not consistently have a human being,

267
00:12:42,210 --> 00:12:45,570
specifically the student's advisor or counselor

268
00:12:45,570 --> 00:12:48,390
in the conversation as well.

269
00:12:48,390 --> 00:12:50,280
We got overconfident and we thought,

270
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,590
oh, AI is capable of answering these things.

271
00:12:52,590 --> 00:12:55,800
But it turns out the superpower of AI

272
00:12:55,800 --> 00:12:59,880
is that people feel vulnerable and open with it.

273
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:01,680
I'm willing to say things to AI

274
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:05,850
that I wouldn't say to a person because I feel embarrassed.

275
00:13:05,850 --> 00:13:06,900
And this is great,

276
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:09,690
but it turns out the flip side of this vulnerability

277
00:13:09,690 --> 00:13:13,500
and non-judgment is actually non-accountability.

278
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:17,070
And without the human being in the conversation as well,

279
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:20,130
if I say, I promise I'll file my FAFSA by next Tuesday

280
00:13:20,130 --> 00:13:23,940
and I don't follow through, I didn't let anybody down.

281
00:13:23,940 --> 00:13:25,440
And so it's very interesting,

282
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:29,520
the temptation with AI is it can do a job,

283
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,340
but I think the next question used to be

284
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:35,070
is that a job that ought to be automated?

285
00:13:35,070 --> 00:13:38,280
Just because it can be done doesn't mean it should be done.

286
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:42,150
And for instance, I guarantee you

287
00:13:42,150 --> 00:13:46,710
Lee's digital twin could conduct today

288
00:13:46,710 --> 00:13:50,700
a 100% flawless wedding ceremony.

289
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:52,800
And I'm sure nobody in this room

290
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:55,290
would want to be married by Lee's digital twin.

291
00:13:55,290 --> 00:13:56,253
Sorry, Lee.

292
00:13:56,253 --> 00:13:57,960
(attendees laughing)

293
00:13:57,960 --> 00:13:59,340
Now, I'm gonna eat my words.

294
00:13:59,340 --> 00:14:01,470
And tomorrow on Google News there's gonna be an article

295
00:14:01,470 --> 00:14:03,570
about some digital twin marrying somebody.

296
00:14:03,570 --> 00:14:06,060
But the fact is just because it can be done

297
00:14:06,060 --> 00:14:07,137
doesn't mean it should be done.

298
00:14:07,137 --> 00:14:10,740
And I think we need to be very cautious and skeptical

299
00:14:10,740 --> 00:14:13,170
and rigorous in our measurement

300
00:14:13,170 --> 00:14:15,990
of what the impacts actually are of certain things.

301
00:14:15,990 --> 00:14:19,533
And maybe they are outstandingly impactful,

302
00:14:20,790 --> 00:14:23,280
or maybe they inadvertently exacerbate problems

303
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:25,380
we don't know until we try.

304
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:28,800
And we have to know and measure carefully

305
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:30,720
to figure out the things that we should scale up,

306
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:33,270
or the things that we should absolutely stop doing.

307
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:35,280
Vinny? - Oh sure.

308
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:37,740
Yeah, thanks, Drew.

309
00:14:37,740 --> 00:14:41,103
So at NetTutor, you know,

310
00:14:42,210 --> 00:14:44,340
I wouldn't say I'm an AI skeptic

311
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:47,370
because we do use AI,

312
00:14:47,370 --> 00:14:49,500
for example, in doing,

313
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:54,180
we can now review all of the sessions using AI,

314
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:56,070
and flag sessions that might be a problem

315
00:14:56,070 --> 00:14:58,770
where five years ago that had to be done very manually,

316
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:00,300
which means we had to take sample sizes

317
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:01,950
and we couldn't really look

318
00:15:01,950 --> 00:15:04,620
at a hundred thousand sessions a week.

319
00:15:04,620 --> 00:15:06,660
And now we can review them all in flag ones

320
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:08,220
that we should go check.

321
00:15:08,220 --> 00:15:10,170
We use AI in our queuing models

322
00:15:10,170 --> 00:15:11,190
and some of other technology.

323
00:15:11,190 --> 00:15:13,830
And of course, as a business we're using AI

324
00:15:13,830 --> 00:15:15,990
for all types of things around sales and marketing,

325
00:15:15,990 --> 00:15:18,483
business administration, data analysis.

326
00:15:19,830 --> 00:15:21,753
When it comes to tutoring, however,

327
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:27,093
we are developing our lobby agent,

328
00:15:28,020 --> 00:15:30,750
you know, if you know much about interaction with students,

329
00:15:30,750 --> 00:15:32,790
in a tutoring model there's a certain number

330
00:15:32,790 --> 00:15:35,287
of minutes required for me to figure out that you,

331
00:15:35,287 --> 00:15:37,110
you know, that I know who you are,

332
00:15:37,110 --> 00:15:39,240
that I can confirm what school you come from.

333
00:15:39,240 --> 00:15:40,410
These are things that are tutors knows,

334
00:15:40,410 --> 00:15:41,550
but we need to confirm, you know,

335
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:43,260
it looks like you came here for algebra.

336
00:15:43,260 --> 00:15:45,420
Sometimes they say, no, I'm here for biology.

337
00:15:45,420 --> 00:15:48,810
And you gotta go back and pick biology, you picked algebra.

338
00:15:48,810 --> 00:15:52,410
And we're pretty confident that that can be done

339
00:15:52,410 --> 00:15:53,810
with a large language model.

340
00:15:54,900 --> 00:15:58,560
And then, so the first 5 to 10 minutes of the session

341
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:01,563
might be able to be done by some type of AI agent.

342
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:04,980
Schools, of course would be allowed to opt into this.

343
00:16:04,980 --> 00:16:07,730
We wouldn't push this on schools as a way to save time.

344
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:12,933
And then, you could be turned over to a live tutor.

345
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:19,360
There's a piece in between

346
00:16:20,430 --> 00:16:24,840
that we're certain, if I kind of know my answer,

347
00:16:24,840 --> 00:16:27,180
I'm just stuck on the, I knew this formula yesterday,

348
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:28,620
I had forgotten it.

349
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:32,310
For sure ChatGPT, some of these other technologies,

350
00:16:32,310 --> 00:16:34,600
that student who's ready to do the work

351
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:37,380
just needs to be reminded.

352
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:40,440
There's probably a really, really good opportunity

353
00:16:40,440 --> 00:16:42,390
for them to get that question answered.

354
00:16:44,070 --> 00:16:45,570
But if you know anything about tutoring,

355
00:16:45,570 --> 00:16:47,490
and this is face-to-face tutoring,

356
00:16:47,490 --> 00:16:50,190
this is advising when you're talking to a student,

357
00:16:50,190 --> 00:16:52,053
and especially online tutoring,

358
00:16:53,010 --> 00:16:54,843
when we say, how can I help you?

359
00:16:56,070 --> 00:16:59,973
A lot of the sessions start with, I don't belong here.

360
00:17:01,470 --> 00:17:04,590
And I do not believe

361
00:17:04,590 --> 00:17:09,030
that we are even close to any type of AI model

362
00:17:09,030 --> 00:17:10,650
that's gonna be able to turn on the video,

363
00:17:10,650 --> 00:17:12,510
look at that student in the eyes and say,

364
00:17:12,510 --> 00:17:15,300
listen, you do belong here and we're gonna do this together.

365
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:16,710
And I know that there's, you know,

366
00:17:16,710 --> 00:17:19,650
empathy being built into AI that says good job.

367
00:17:19,650 --> 00:17:22,620
And I heard, somebody was telling me that they were so proud

368
00:17:22,620 --> 00:17:25,530
that AI is teaching students to say please and thank you.

369
00:17:25,530 --> 00:17:27,900
That's all good and dandy and I support it all.

370
00:17:27,900 --> 00:17:29,190
And I think that stuff is great.

371
00:17:29,190 --> 00:17:32,610
And by the way, we also use AI for our support bot.

372
00:17:32,610 --> 00:17:34,350
If you want to get tech support from us,

373
00:17:34,350 --> 00:17:36,150
you know, we think we can get your question answered.

374
00:17:36,150 --> 00:17:38,003
If not, then you can ask for an agent.

375
00:17:38,850 --> 00:17:40,290
But the types of things

376
00:17:40,290 --> 00:17:42,210
that happen in a real tutoring session,

377
00:17:42,210 --> 00:17:43,983
the type of rapport you build,

378
00:17:45,510 --> 00:17:50,040
I don't think we're close to being able to say.

379
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:52,080
And by the way, replace tutor with teacher

380
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:54,150
and all the teachers in the room will agree,

381
00:17:54,150 --> 00:17:55,920
you know, AI can't teach.

382
00:17:55,920 --> 00:17:57,300
AI can give you some instruction,

383
00:17:57,300 --> 00:17:59,040
AI can ask you some questions,

384
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:02,313
but AI cannot do what a teacher does.

385
00:18:03,300 --> 00:18:04,470
And so that's where we sort of,

386
00:18:04,470 --> 00:18:06,600
and that tutor just kind of draw that line

387
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:07,860
and we're gonna go up to it

388
00:18:07,860 --> 00:18:09,720
and maybe we'll go a little bit beyond it,

389
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:10,620
but at the end of the day,

390
00:18:10,620 --> 00:18:13,170
we think that there's a human element

391
00:18:13,170 --> 00:18:17,700
that it's not gonna go away for a while,

392
00:18:17,700 --> 00:18:18,753
maybe someday.

393
00:18:18,753 --> 00:18:20,490
(attendees applauding)

394
00:18:20,490 --> 00:18:21,570
- Amen and hallelujah.

395
00:18:21,570 --> 00:18:25,320
I would also add what is the job of a teacher?

396
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:26,790
Because it's not only,

397
00:18:26,790 --> 00:18:28,980
and we talked about this earlier, Vinny,

398
00:18:28,980 --> 00:18:31,950
not only being a subject matter expert,

399
00:18:31,950 --> 00:18:33,150
I mean obviously people

400
00:18:33,150 --> 00:18:35,910
are incredibly decorated to be faculty,

401
00:18:35,910 --> 00:18:40,110
but the great teachers are the ones that motivate learners

402
00:18:40,110 --> 00:18:43,170
to struggle a little bit more than they would on their own.

403
00:18:43,170 --> 00:18:47,220
And that's second part is truly extraordinary

404
00:18:47,220 --> 00:18:49,890
and really requires a human connection

405
00:18:49,890 --> 00:18:51,750
and trust and empathy,

406
00:18:51,750 --> 00:18:55,260
which AI might be able to mimic but can never replace.

407
00:18:55,260 --> 00:18:59,400
And we just need to be very careful about the relationships

408
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:03,300
that we are developing and students are developing with AI.

409
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:07,410
It's worth noting that the number one use case,

410
00:19:07,410 --> 00:19:08,610
it was number two last year

411
00:19:08,610 --> 00:19:12,420
and it just became number one this year for Gen Z

412
00:19:12,420 --> 00:19:16,080
is to use AI for companionship,

413
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:18,570
like the social replacement.

414
00:19:18,570 --> 00:19:20,610
And they turn to it when they have good news

415
00:19:20,610 --> 00:19:22,140
or they have a bad day.

416
00:19:22,140 --> 00:19:26,100
And it is very interesting, possibly therapeutic,

417
00:19:26,100 --> 00:19:29,550
but we've been down this road before with social media

418
00:19:29,550 --> 00:19:31,470
where we thought we had a technology

419
00:19:31,470 --> 00:19:32,820
that was going to bring us together

420
00:19:32,820 --> 00:19:35,760
and it inadvertently driven us apart.

421
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:37,560
I think we should be very careful

422
00:19:37,560 --> 00:19:41,130
and scrutinize what this technology is doing

423
00:19:41,130 --> 00:19:42,720
to our social connections.

424
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:45,660
And so, you're right, like, anything that prevents us

425
00:19:45,660 --> 00:19:48,780
from being human beings together

426
00:19:48,780 --> 00:19:50,573
should be taken with a grain of salt.

427
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:52,530
- Thank you for that.

428
00:19:52,530 --> 00:19:54,750
I'd like to expand on that just a little bit

429
00:19:54,750 --> 00:19:56,550
and open it up to the panel.

430
00:19:56,550 --> 00:19:58,470
Just the concept of ethics.

431
00:19:58,470 --> 00:20:01,350
We always, I always get questions about, like, ethics,

432
00:20:01,350 --> 00:20:03,510
and especially when it comes to like avatars

433
00:20:03,510 --> 00:20:04,830
and digital twins.

434
00:20:04,830 --> 00:20:09,270
So, you know, in your own personal expertise,

435
00:20:09,270 --> 00:20:13,023
how, what are your thoughts on AI and ethics?

436
00:20:15,330 --> 00:20:16,923
It's open to anyone. - Yeah.

437
00:20:18,624 --> 00:20:20,550
- Well, I would just like to comment a little bit

438
00:20:20,550 --> 00:20:22,950
about on ethical use of AI,

439
00:20:22,950 --> 00:20:25,080
because when ChatGPT first came out,

440
00:20:25,080 --> 00:20:26,700
the very very first thing we had to do

441
00:20:26,700 --> 00:20:30,090
was reach out to all 500, 600 of our customers and say,

442
00:20:30,090 --> 00:20:31,890
what is your AI policy?

443
00:20:31,890 --> 00:20:36,210
And of course, 599 of them said, AI policy?

444
00:20:36,210 --> 00:20:37,610
Should we have one of those?

445
00:20:38,670 --> 00:20:42,360
Typing three prompts into ChatGPT,

446
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:45,690
taking what it spits out, copying and pasting the word,

447
00:20:45,690 --> 00:20:47,640
and submitting it as your work is plagiarism, right?

448
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:50,430
Nobody argues that, I think we all know that.

449
00:20:50,430 --> 00:20:52,200
I sat for the CPA exam in the eighties

450
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:54,000
and I wasn't allowed to use a calculator

451
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:58,950
because accountants should know how to do the math.

452
00:20:58,950 --> 00:21:00,300
How dumb does that sound now, right?

453
00:21:00,300 --> 00:21:03,180
Like, that was, this is not what we expect.

454
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:05,520
And so, ethical use of AI

455
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:08,790
is part of what we train our tutors to do using AI.

456
00:21:08,790 --> 00:21:11,870
So for example, asking ChatGPT to help you,

457
00:21:11,870 --> 00:21:12,840
you know, I have to write this,

458
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:14,580
can you give me some suggestions on prompts

459
00:21:14,580 --> 00:21:15,930
or some sources that I might get

460
00:21:15,930 --> 00:21:16,920
so where I can go do my work.

461
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:18,540
I mean, I think that's a good thing.

462
00:21:18,540 --> 00:21:20,123
Some colleges may still prohibit that.

463
00:21:20,123 --> 00:21:22,110
I mean, it's a college by college thing,

464
00:21:22,110 --> 00:21:26,160
but so I think there's,

465
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:30,600
to me, yeah, there's always the right way

466
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:31,740
to do things.

467
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:33,570
Like, at the end of the day, if you think about,

468
00:21:33,570 --> 00:21:35,340
there's a lot of ways to get there.

469
00:21:35,340 --> 00:21:38,070
And I think students know,

470
00:21:38,070 --> 00:21:40,833
and I think teachers know and I think tutors know-

471
00:21:42,134 --> 00:21:46,350
- And that to an AI front end and have that dialogue.

472
00:21:46,350 --> 00:21:48,900
What is your context, what is your direction,

473
00:21:48,900 --> 00:21:51,150
why do you want to know the answer to that?

474
00:21:51,150 --> 00:21:54,330
So, if a student asks me, I don't get it.

475
00:21:54,330 --> 00:21:56,520
I mean, atoms are fundamentally empty.

476
00:21:56,520 --> 00:22:00,060
So how are they hard and how could they weight so much?

477
00:22:00,060 --> 00:22:02,460
And if I'm typically in lecture, thinking,

478
00:22:02,460 --> 00:22:04,830
oh, God, this is a digression, right?

479
00:22:04,830 --> 00:22:07,980
I turn that into a prompt and I teach the entire class.

480
00:22:07,980 --> 00:22:10,290
There's actually an interesting piece here,

481
00:22:10,290 --> 00:22:12,030
so let's have that conversation.

482
00:22:12,030 --> 00:22:14,340
But when you go, as Michelle has said,

483
00:22:14,340 --> 00:22:18,270
you don't ask an AI engine, why are atoms empty?

484
00:22:18,270 --> 00:22:22,050
You might ask, what are they made of if they can be hard

485
00:22:22,050 --> 00:22:24,120
and empty at the same time?

486
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:25,830
And turn that into a dialogue,

487
00:22:25,830 --> 00:22:28,773
and then explore the composition of an atom.

488
00:22:30,030 --> 00:22:31,740
- Yeah, just a quick follow up

489
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:34,590
that also when I think in the classroom,

490
00:22:34,590 --> 00:22:38,130
a lot of teacher kind of facing, try to accuse student

491
00:22:38,130 --> 00:22:41,460
of plagiarism or cheating because they have no control.

492
00:22:41,460 --> 00:22:43,860
The way that I find that work out is,

493
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:45,720
I teach engineering graphics, right?

494
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:47,520
Solid work, it's a theory.

495
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:50,490
And those theory can be very, if I give like a 10-question,

496
00:22:50,490 --> 00:22:52,140
like a final or a midterm, and say,

497
00:22:52,140 --> 00:22:54,690
hey, describe what's orthographic view,

498
00:22:54,690 --> 00:22:58,230
describe, you know, what is the thread, blah, blah, blah.

499
00:22:58,230 --> 00:23:01,200
Those things can be done in five minutes just feeding to GPT

500
00:23:01,200 --> 00:23:03,180
and then come back and everything's done.

501
00:23:03,180 --> 00:23:04,710
But I didn't do that.

502
00:23:04,710 --> 00:23:06,597
I said, we are gonna have oral exam,

503
00:23:06,597 --> 00:23:09,180
and you can go look at GPT,

504
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:10,950
you can do modeling, you can write anything,

505
00:23:10,950 --> 00:23:14,190
but the exam, you stand up there

506
00:23:14,190 --> 00:23:16,050
and you explain to me verbally.

507
00:23:16,050 --> 00:23:18,960
If you can remember all of the theory, you are done.

508
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:20,610
I don't care how you study it.

509
00:23:20,610 --> 00:23:21,443
So we have to...

510
00:23:21,443 --> 00:23:23,670
I kind of felt like I tell myself I have to change

511
00:23:23,670 --> 00:23:27,840
the way that how I teach to accommodate technology

512
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:29,440
and what's available to student.

513
00:23:30,570 --> 00:23:31,680
- That's great.

514
00:23:31,680 --> 00:23:34,860
At Mainstay we have like our own internal,

515
00:23:34,860 --> 00:23:37,320
we call it our hippocratic oath of AI.

516
00:23:37,320 --> 00:23:40,080
I think we've had it for like five or six years.

517
00:23:40,080 --> 00:23:42,030
And it's pretty simple actually.

518
00:23:42,030 --> 00:23:43,500
And I'd encourage you to just think

519
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:45,210
about the principles for yourself,

520
00:23:45,210 --> 00:23:48,480
what standards you wanna hold yourselves

521
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:49,710
